A||||12||William||easy out not so easy!   (or naked chick pics) :-)||||1||
Z||000000||William||03-17-2001||04:28 PM||||Has anybody in the history of the earth every get a bolt out using an extractor? Day one of the big suspension rebuild started great. I ve never done anything like this but I was flying! Got the sway bar off, popped loose the outer tie rods, out came the rack and pinion, all in 30 minutes. Then it happened! I snapped off one of the rack mounting bolts. I ran and purchased an easy out and bit, drilled out the bolt and promptly broke the extractor off in the bolt. The extractor will not drill out. What can I possibly do now? It started off so good and now the day is shot. I've been messing with the bolt for 5 hours now.  :-(||||reg||1
Z||000001||rockcrawl||03-17-2001||05:05 PM||||The rack mounts are accesible from the back, just torch off the chassis nut and weld a new one on. If you don't have a torch and welder, cut or chisel it off and just use a wrench on the nut when you reassemble it.<P>Must have been a cheap screw extractor. Try to use a good one like Hanson next time.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||William||03-17-2001||05:32 PM||||The extractor was a hanson but I think it was too small. Will it really be possible to chisel though a weld? That would seem to be a impossible task? luckly it is the front nut that is the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||William||03-17-2001||09:04 PM||||No luck on the chisel idea and the space is to tight for my dremel tool. Night fall has come and the bolt is still in there. Any ideas? I can't beleive i'm gonna have to spend hundreds on a accetelene torch setup to get this freakin bolt out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jake_Dragon||03-17-2001||10:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by William:<BR><B>No luck on the chisel idea and the space is to tight for my dremel tool. Night fall has come and the bolt is still in there. Any ideas? I can't beleive i'm gonna have to spend hundreds on a accetelene torch setup to get this freakin bolt out.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Don't try and break the entire weld at once. Start at one end and run your chisel slowly across the weld.<BR>Once you have a grove across the weld just keep working it back and forth weakening it a little at a time.<BR>A two pound hammer helps as well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">   <BR> <IMG SRC="http://home1.gte.net/rebel33/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||GoCartGuy||03-18-2001||12:48 AM||||Before you go and buy a torch and go cutting, try the chisel idea again.  As a tid bit, heat the weld a bit.  Run by the hardware store and spend $30 on a propane torch like you use for plumbing.  Just keep a good direct heat on it for a few minutes and it'll weaken the weld.  Then go after it with the chisel, but be gentle, you don't want to damage the surrounding area.  Just some advice from my old shop teacher.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||03-18-2001||09:54 AM||||I feel your pain: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006261.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006261.html</A> <P>Is this a special day for broken fasteners?  Are we under a curse because we're trying to work on our cars instead of getting knee-walking drunk like the rest of the Celtic-influenced world?<P>Anyhow, about your problem . . .  Is there room to use a Dremel cutting disk to sever the weld?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||BENGAL4||03-18-2001||11:17 AM||||You don't have to buy an oxy/acety outfit. You should be able to rent one fairly cheep. A propane torch, will not weaken the weld because it doesn't get hot enough. My company rents torch set ups, welders ect. We have a location in Orlando. Give me a call tomorrow and I will give you the Forum discount. 1-800-333-2151 ext 495.<P>Bill<BR>86GT 3800 II S/C||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||03-18-2001||12:28 PM||||I bought 6 dremel tool "blue" stone grinding tips when I broken an easy-out trying to remove my swaybar mount. Took about 20 minutes and 3 stones to grind thru the easy-out, Then I drilled the hole out all the way and used a bolt and nut.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||03-18-2001||01:27 PM||||Get the flex shaft tool for the dremel. That thing should reach it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||William||03-18-2001||04:16 PM||||Thank you for the replys. To be clear,I can get to the nut with the tool itself but the nut is on the underside of the skinny u shaped bracket so the cutter wheel cuts the bracket before it gets to the nut. Also, thanks for the offer of the rental but orlando id way to far from me. I'm going to try a combination of carbide bits and grinding stones. Hope it works out, Got 30 minutes into this month long project and already hit a snag. Can't wait to get the those strut bolts!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000011||skitime||03-18-2001||07:20 PM||||Screw extractors and taps are very high carbon steel and are very hard and brittle. You can use that to your advantage by hitting it with a quality center punch. It will cause the extractor to break into little pieces till you get all the pieces out.<P>Good Luck||||reg||1
